From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Mon, 13 Jun 1994 11:40:24 -0800
Subject: [#94-6-40] SCI: Chaos, Complexity, and Reductionism

Regarding the recent discussion of the proper use or meaning of the term
"emergent phenomena":

Here is a summary of a recent technical report from the Santa Fe Institute,
"The Evolution of Emergent Computation", by James P. Crutchfield and
Melanie Mitchell (SFI tech report 94-03-012).  I have no connection to the
Santa Fe Institute or to either of the authors, but I think they have done
a good job at carefully defining their specific use of the term "emergent"
in a way that could shed light on the general discussion.  My intent in
this post is purely illustrative -- to show how the term "emergent" can be
given a clear and definite meaning in this particular example.

The authors study one-dimensional cellular automata (CA).  Each cell
changes its state as a function of its three nearest neighbors in either
direction (the states are binary, and the one dimensional array "wraps
around" at the ends -- i.e. it's really a circle).  The CA is initialized
with some random configuration of states, 1s and 0s, and the computational
task is to decide whether or not the initial configuration consists of more
than half 1s.  The desired behavior is for the CA to quickly converge to a
state of all 1s if the initial state consists of more than half 1s, and to
converge to all 0s otherwise.  This task is interesting because it is
intrinsically global in nature -- in the authors' words, "no linear
combination of local computations -- such as the cells computing the
majority of 1s in their neighborhood -- can solve this problem."  The
authors' employ a genetic algorithm to evolve CA for performing this task.

The resulting CAs which perform best share a fascinating property:  their
strictly local rules give rise to information carrying "particles" which
propagate through the array with varying rates, and interact with each
other when they collide.  These "particles" consist of the location of
boundaries or "interfaces" between regions of the array with different
regular repeated patterns.  Different computational strategies discovered
by different CA can be completely analyzed by an identification of the
types of particles that can evolve, their directions and speeds of
propagation, and the rules for interaction that describe what happens when
two particles collide (for example, they may annihilate each other, they
may generate several new particles of different types, they may merge into
a single new particle, etc.)  It is like a kind of "particle physics" in
the "active media" of the cellular array.

Although the individual elements of the cellular automata, and their rules,
and even the genetic algorithm used for evolving them are all "local" in
nature, these particles have the ability to propagate over large space-time
distances and conduct a qualitatively different type of computation than
the local rules.  They can carry information about the density of 1s and 0s
from distant parts of the array.  These particles, and the computation
which they carry out, are "emergent" from the local elements and state
transition function of the cellular automata.  The term "emergent" here is
not offered as a further clarification of what is going on, but simply as a
label that seems easy to agree should be applied in this case.  (The
meaning of a term resides in its agreed upon usage.)

(To retrieve postscript of this tech report from the Santa Fe Institute,
ftp to ftp.santafe.edu, cd to /pub/Users/mm, and get file EvEmComp.ps.Z)

This is my first posting to the list.  Greetings to all!

