------------------------------------------------------------------------


Having followed the discussion on quantum computing on this
newsgroup for some time now, I would like to hazard my own
opinion on the matter. In discussions like this
it is often implied that continued miniaturization of electron
devices will essentially force industry to adopt
completely coherent quantum computers, within the next
two to three decades. This may be necessary if one restricts
attention to semiconductor-based technologies. Even then
Landauer (last reference below) has pointed out problems such as 
wave function localization, which will limit the use of abstract 
models of coherent computation which have been
implemented on condensed matter systems. Another problem 
with coherent computing is the need for extremely low 
temperatures. However, there are a number of recent proposals for
"hardware" implementations of quantum computers :

@article{lloyd93:ar,
Author = {S. Lloyd},
title = {A Potentially Realizable Quantum Computer},
journal = {Science},
year = 1993,
volume = 261,
pages = {1569--1571},
month = {September}  }

@article{bando94:ar,
Author = {S. Bandyopadhyay and B. Das and A. E. Miller},
title = {Supercomputing with Spin Polarized Single Electrons
in a Quantum Coupled Architechture},
journal = {to appear in Nanotechnology},
year = 1994,
pages = {},
month = {}  }

@article{lent93:ar,
Author = {C. S. Lent and P. D. Tougaw and W. Porod and G. H. Bernstein},
title = {Quantum Cellular Automata},
journal = {Nanotechnology},
year = 1993,
volume = 4,
pages = {49--57},
month = {}  }

@article{korner93:ar,
Author = {H. K\"{o}rner and G. Mahler},
title = {Optically Driven Quantum Networks : Applications
in Molecular Electronics},
journal = {Phys. Rev. B},
year = 1993,
volume = 48,
number = 4,
pages = {2335--2346},
month = {July}  }

These have been commented on in the following :

@incollection{landauer94:ar,
Author = {Rolf Landauer},
title = {Is Quantum Mechanics Useful},
booktitle = {Ultimate Limits of Fabrication and Measurement},
editor = {M. E. Welland},
publisher = {Kluwer},
address = {Dordrecht},
year = 1994,
pages = {} }

Opinion :

There are probably alternative approaches which will permit 
the use of semiclassical models well into the nanometer 
level of device scaling, while allowing room temperature
operation. One such effect is the single-electron phenomenon,
which comes about when the transfer of a single electron
elicits a substantial change in the electrostatic potential
of a nanometer-sized particle. A metallic particle 5 nm in
diameter can have over 1000 conduction electrons, and can be
treated semiclassically. Recently, a memory device based on this effect 
was demonstrated at room temperature (K. Yano et. al., Hitachi). 
There is reason to believe that large arrays of such particles can 
be configured to achieve limited computational effects.
How these might compare with coherent computing in terms
of performance measures, it is premature to comment.

[Moderator's note - Thanks for providing a reference to the Seth Lloyd
paper, and thanks for the others, too! - jb]
